WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:10.180
Hey Wisecrack, Jared here.&nbsp;

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.100
You may have noticed a trend lately.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:16.890
It seems like every day, some company announces
a new cloud or platform service for people

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:17.890
to download.&nbsp;

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:22.150
A few months ago Google announced its Google
Stadia game streaming platform, which will

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:26.400
include YouTube integration, and allow gamers
to play games from most any screen with an

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.490
internet connection.&nbsp;

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:31.720
The announcement was met with some excitement
from big game developers looking for new distribution

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:37.010
deals… and... a lot of skepticism from gamers,
prompting eyerolls and also some questions

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.210
like, what games will be available?

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.260
Will people actually own their games or will
everything be subscription based?

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.100
How will everyone’s data be used?&nbsp;

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.800
And what will happen when Google takes on
Sony, Nintendo, Microsoft… and popular PC

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.700
gaming platforms like Steam?

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:56.320
It’s not just gaming platforms that have
people asking questions, though.&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.960
“Platforms” and “subscription models”
are spreading like the plague.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:04.879
CBS has successfully forced nerds into submission
by requiring them to pay 5.99 a month for

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:08.670
the privilege of watching Star Trek on their
“All-Access” platform.&nbsp;

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:13.120
Apple recently announced that devices will
be taking a backseat to game, news, and video

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:15.720
subscriptions on a new Apple platform.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:21.060
And Disney, who already owns… a lot of things
… is launching Disney+, which will ask us

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.420
to pay another subscription to watch things
that were once available on Netflix and a

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:27.220
slate of new Marvel shows.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:32.110
And NBC has yet to launch their own streaming
service which will, again, steal The Office

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:42.360
from Netflix.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.369
People seem annoyed that these services are
just another thing hankering to siphon a few

00:01:46.369 --> 00:01:48.229
bucks a month out of their pockets.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:50.460
But is there a deeper problem here?

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.650
And is that problem platforms themselves?

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:57.590
Maybe, maybe not, but for better or worse,
platforms are changing our entire economic

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:02.330
system and I’m here to talk about how, why,
and what it all means.&nbsp;

00:02:02.330 --> 00:03:04.299
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the platform
economy.&nbsp;

00:03:04.299 --> 00:03:16.400
Today we’re surrounded by platforms.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.680
A Platform is a website or application that
brings two or more sides of a market together,

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.569
that is, a digital space where people who
need a service can find and pay for that service.

00:03:26.569 --> 00:03:28.689
The earliest example is probably eBay.

00:03:28.689 --> 00:03:33.569
The site originally launched as AuctionWeb
in 1995, and created a platform for someone

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:37.670
who wants to sell their autographed Marky
Mark underwear to an eager buyer, this is

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:38.909
real by the way.

00:03:38.909 --> 00:03:42.909
After eBay’s success, other companies started
springing up with similar business models--

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:47.780
Amazon originally connected book publishers
with readers --remember when amazon only sold

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:48.780
books?

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:53.639
Ticketmaster created an online space for venues
to sell tickets, and soon there were platforms

00:03:53.639 --> 00:03:59.129
for everything… now Uber, connects drivers
with passengers, AirBnB connects spare rooms

00:03:59.129 --> 00:04:03.180
with travelers, and Spotify, connects listeners
with music.&nbsp;

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.700
But let’s back up for a minute-- how did
we even get here?

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:11.400
Once upon a time before free markets, economies
were regionally based, and self contained.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.139
According to Political Economist Karl Polanyi,
there were three general types of economic

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:17.139
systems:&nbsp;

00:04:17.139 --> 00:04:20.290
Reciprocity, where goods are exchanged as
gifts between communities.

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:25.080
For instance, a tribe gifts another tribe
a cow, and eventually gets one back later

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.580
on in the year.&nbsp;

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:32.099
Householding, in which families produce food,
goods, and tools for their own use and consumption.

00:04:32.099 --> 00:04:37.280
And Redistribution, where a tribal leader
or feudal lord controls all the resources,

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:39.880
and then redistributes them to members of
their society.

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.069
Now remember this one, we’re going to come
back to it later.&nbsp;

00:04:43.069 --> 00:04:46.949
In all of these, survival wasn’t really
based on how efficient production was.

00:04:46.949 --> 00:04:52.110
Sure, a crop may not grow well year over year,
but there were contingency plans such as storing

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:56.660
crops, or minimal exchange on simple markets,
like bartering with your neighbor for what

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:57.660
you need.

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:01.060
But eventually capitalism happened, and the
rules changed.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.830
People became increasingly separated from
the things they needed to survive, turning

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:09.770
to the market to get things like food, water,
and shelter, and selling their skills to whoever

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:10.770
would pay.&nbsp;

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:15.590
By the late 1700s, industry was booming, and
manufacturing began to take over Europe.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:20.910
In 1776, Scottish economist Adam Smith published
An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:25.509
Wealth of Nations, outlining the benefits
of efficient production and singing praises

00:05:25.509 --> 00:05:27.850
about how dope the free market is.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.560
And people liked his theories.

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:34.120
The market eventually expanded, as standards
of living began to change, from bare essentials

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.150
like food and clothes to luxury items.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:41.610
But having so many different sellers selling
all these things led to competitive pressures.

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:44.580
If your goods were too expensive, no one would
buy them.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.810
So businesses started lowering production
costs so they could sell their items for less,

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.259
while still maintaining enough profits.&nbsp;

00:05:51.259 --> 00:05:56.379
Businesses have been lowering production costs
ever since, primarily by turning to technology,

00:05:56.379 --> 00:05:58.300
and lowering labor costs.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:02.599
Which was easy to do if you were in manufacturing
as technology got better and better, and you

00:06:02.599 --> 00:06:04.949
could pay less and less for labor.&nbsp;

00:06:04.949 --> 00:06:09.000
It started with assembly lines, which provided
companies with cheaper labor that didn’t

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.250
need to be trained in any special skills—
instead of learning how to put a whole car

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:17.980
together, a worker just had to learn how to
put one piece of the car in one place.&nbsp;

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:22.110
Eventually, large business realized that robots
could do that instead of people, and since

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:26.629
you don’t have to pay robots salaries, soon
some of these places barely had any employees

00:06:26.629 --> 00:06:27.629
at all.&nbsp;

00:06:27.629 --> 00:06:31.280
But there were still businesses that couldn’t
really use assembly lines or robots to cut

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.289
as many costs— places like grocery stores,
or taxi companies, or movie theaters.

00:06:36.289 --> 00:06:38.470
Until the internet came around.&nbsp;

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:42.889
Stores and service providers suddenly had
to compete with… websites, where you could

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:47.159
get the same product or service mailed to
you directly from the distributor with the

00:06:47.159 --> 00:06:51.780
click of a button, or find a room in someone’s
house to sleep in instead of booking a hotel

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:52.780
room.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:57.500
The owners of these new websites, these platforms,
don’t need to pay any labor costs because

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:00.449
people doing the buying and selling are doing
it for them.

00:07:00.449 --> 00:07:05.849
In other words, Uber isn’t a taxi company,
it’s a technology company, that pairs an

00:07:05.849 --> 00:07:09.889
independently contracted driver with you,
the consumer.

00:07:09.889 --> 00:07:14.870
But even those independently contracted drivers
are a liability, as the company moves towards&nbsp;

00:07:14.870 --> 00:07:15.970
driverless vehicles.&nbsp;

00:07:15.970 --> 00:07:21.319
Okay, so now we understand how platforms happened,
but why are they making people so uneasy?

00:07:21.319 --> 00:07:25.770
Well, it might be because people are losing
their sense of autonomy and ownership.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:29.479
You know how I asked you to remember Karl
Polanyi’s redistribution, where one person

00:07:29.479 --> 00:07:32.449
controls and redistributes all the resources?

00:07:32.449 --> 00:07:33.470
Like a feudal lord?

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:37.669
Well it’s because more and more, the way
platforms operate feels very similar to that

00:07:37.669 --> 00:07:38.729
kind of economy.&nbsp;

00:07:38.729 --> 00:07:43.669
Tech companies own assets and distribute them
to us, and as a result, some have argued a

00:07:43.669 --> 00:07:45.990
new kind of feudalism has emerged.&nbsp;

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:51.700
One where companies are like lords, and subscribers
are like serfs paying rent to access some

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:52.700
stuff.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:56.080
So if this is the case, will we all be indentured
to our platforms?&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:01.340
In his 2016 article Evgeny Morozov describes
the steady march of Google and Facebook toward

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:04.740
feudal ownership over greater proportions
of the economy.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:09.220
By “[destroying] all competition and [making]
the world dependent on their platforms,”

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:14.360
Morozov suggests these neo-feudal lords will
increase their control over how economic resources

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:15.560
are accessed.

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:19.490
Political theorist Nick Srnicek goes as far
as saying that these big companies are becoming

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:24.460
“owners of the infrastructures of society...ending
private property for the masses and having

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:28.980
everything provided as-a-service by quasi-monopolistic
firms.”&nbsp;

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:33.740
We don’t own stuff anymore, companies basically
lease everything to us, and at the moment

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:37.530
these companies are all fighting with each
other over who gets to provide us with access

00:08:37.530 --> 00:08:39.600
to stuff that they control.&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:43.520
Instead of owning cars, people are taking
ride shares with changing prices generated

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:44.950
by algorithms.

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:49.100
Instead of owning CDs, we get our music from
Spotify, where it can be taken down.&nbsp;

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:53.150
And it gets even more complicated when these
different platforms start fighting each other

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:55.310
over who has access to what.&nbsp;

00:08:55.310 --> 00:09:00.510
Back in January Epic games announced an exclusivity
deal with Metro: Exodus just 2 weeks before

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:05.170
the game’s launch-- after many people had
already pre-ordered it on Steam-- sparking

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:09.690
backlash from Steam users, who wanted their
money back, and Steam itself, who wanted to

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:10.960
sell the game.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:14.871
If you want to watch The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel
you need an Amazon Prime subscription, if

00:09:14.871 --> 00:09:18.590
you want to watch Stranger Things you need
a Netflix subscription, if you want to watch

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:22.590
Star Trek Discovery… well… hopefully you
can borrow a CBS all access password from

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:23.600
your dad.

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:28.090
But the point is people are shelling out money
to all these platforms for temporary access

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:31.500
to a tv show instead of buying a box set.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.800
Remember when you just had to choose between
dvd and blu-ray?

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:38.640
While it’s annoying enough to have 37 different
logins, and frustrating to use up hard drive

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:44.140
space for some dumb software you only need
for one game there are pretty serious ramifications

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:45.770
to these new ownership models.

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:50.250
Last year farmers lost their right to repair
tractors and equipment they bought from John

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:51.250
Deere.

00:09:51.250 --> 00:09:56.030
Under a new agreement, farmers can no longer
access or modify the source code of any farm

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:58.740
equipment software, or change engine settings.

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.930
They can’t even purchase repair parts without
going through an authorized dealer.

00:10:02.930 --> 00:10:05.160
Which you can find through their app.&nbsp;

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:09.530
This is a big deal for farmers, who have been
modifying and fixing their own equipment since

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:10.530
forever.

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:14.410
If an important piece of farm equipment breaks,
they can’t always afford to wait for a John

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:17.350
Deere rep to come update their software for
them.

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:21.430
And not being allowed to upgrade their tractors
means they can’t update their systems to

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:25.721
meet future emissions requirements, which
means when standards change… they’ll have

00:10:25.721 --> 00:10:27.600
to buy a new tractor.&nbsp;

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.410
This isn’t new, it’s just spreading.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:35.330
Without software, our smartphones, which have
become pretty indispensable, are just fancy

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:36.330
boxes.

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:37.760
We lease that software.

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:44.140
“Leasing software” isn’t anything new
either, as Microsoft pushes Office 365 subscriptions,

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:46.550
or Adobe doing basically the same thing.

00:10:46.550 --> 00:10:50.450
And we’re at a point where we’re literally
advocating for laws so that we can fix things

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:52.030
that we already own.

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:55.690
The ‘right to repair’ movement has already
been picking up steam and appealing to the

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:57.740
US government to address these issues.&nbsp;

00:10:57.740 --> 00:11:03.050
So, as big companies like Apple and Google
and Disney take over platforms and buy everyone

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:08.230
out, we might finally have fewer passwords
to memorize and fewer apps to purchase-- but

00:11:08.230 --> 00:11:09.430
at what cost?&nbsp;

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:11.960
To put it bluntly: the cost is our data.&nbsp;

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:16.560
According to Srnicek, data “have become
increasingly central to firms and their relations

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:19.800
with workers, customers, and other capitalists.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.730
Platforms became an efficient way to monopolize,
extract, analyze, and use the increasingly

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:26.870
large amounts of data that were being recorded.”&nbsp;

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:31.480
So, while some of these businesses operate
by skimming off the top of sales, or charging

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:36.270
subscription fees, a portion of their money
and success actually comes from collecting,

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:38.630
using, and selling your information.

00:11:38.630 --> 00:11:42.530
And these platforms are being increasingly
integrated into our daily lives.

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:47.000
Uber calculates fares based on information
coming in through the app, Spotify recommends

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:50.930
new music based on an analysis of what you’ve
been listening to…&nbsp; and then there are

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:55.770
companies who are flat out selling information
for profit to third party data brokers…

00:11:55.770 --> 00:12:00.610
who forward it on to ad agencies, insurance
brokers, and anyone else who wants it.

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:05.040
Kind of like how Rockefeller owned all the
oil, big tech companies monopolizing on data

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:09.070
are starting to take over sections of the
economy, providing valuable services that

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:13.760
people won’t stop paying for…and making
it difficult for anyone else to provide those

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:14.760
services.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:18.830
What’s different is instead of controlling
physical resources, these businesses control

00:12:18.830 --> 00:12:21.400
our information and how its used.

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:25.630
Netflix has so much information on people’s
viewing habits, they can keep recommending

00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:29.330
shows for people to watch so they’ll keep
their subscriptions active.&nbsp;

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:34.410
So it goes beyond selling your search history
to advertisers: companies use data to wield

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:39.700
a huge amount of power when it comes to shaping
our consumption, or even the economy as a

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:40.700
whole.

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:41.770
Take Google Stadia.

00:12:41.770 --> 00:12:46.410
An article from Brian Feldman in the Intelligencer
points out that linking Stadia so closely

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:50.850
with YouTube might create a feedback loop
of engagement metrics that could ultimately

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:52.830
influence game-design choices.

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:56.600
According to Feldman, “it’s not hard to
imagine a world in which the Stadia platform

00:12:56.600 --> 00:13:01.190
ends up wielding major, indirect influence
over what types of things game developers

00:13:01.190 --> 00:13:04.130
create and what YouTubers decide to stream.”&nbsp;

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:09.120
With everyone chasing trends, some game developers
might just end up creating cookie-cutter games

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:10.770
that all look the same.&nbsp;

00:13:10.770 --> 00:13:16.070
We see it with videos, like how when one watched
conspiracy theory videos, YouTube suggested

00:13:16.070 --> 00:13:21.190
more conspiracy videos, leading to an increase
in more conspiracy content, leading to more

00:13:21.190 --> 00:13:24.990
conspiracy theorists, who wanted more conspiracy
content.

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:29.020
They’ve since fixed that, but you’re usually
only one Mukbang video away from the topic

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:30.690
dominating your homepage.&nbsp;

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:32.700
But are we getting ahead of ourselves?&nbsp;

00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:36.800
More than a century ago, policymakers were
confronted with a very similar problem, when

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:39.100
people started using telephones.

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:43.370
People owned their telephones, but they didn’t
own the system they were using to communicate.

00:13:43.370 --> 00:13:47.480
As people began to rely on telephones for
communication, phone companies turned into

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:49.630
big unregulated monopolies.

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:54.320
So, the government started placing restrictions
on the companies to keep them from exploiting

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:59.130
consumers, allowing them to be monopolies,
but treating them as utilities.

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:03.680
So maybe the federal trade commission will
do something similar with tech platforms…

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:08.860
some policymakers even propose creating new
departments in government to oversee big platforms.

00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:13.380
While it’s still unclear what that will
look like, with Uber drivers striking to unionize

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:18.130
and Mark Zuckerberg testifying before congress,
it seems like calls for intervention will

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:19.430
only increase

00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:20.470
So what do you think?

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:24.680
Are we destined for lives in the Matrix where
our data is extracted so big companies can

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:29.020
profit trillions of dollars and dictate everything
we do with our time?

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:33.550
Or will we find a middle ground where we live
harmoniously with our platforms in some kind

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:34.830
of data-driven utopia?

00:14:34.830 --> 00:14:36.380
Let us know what you think.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:39.470
Thanks to all our patrons who support the
channel and our podcasts.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:41.410
Don’t forget to hit that subscribe button

